% === provenance: IsomottonenCochez2014 ===
% CLAIM: Students learning Git confuse its concepts -- e.g., conceptualize
%   branches as directories and try to `cd` into them -- and this difficulty
%   is partly attributable to the command-line client; visual diagrams of the
%   commit graph help.  The same source documents further command-level
%   confusions: `git add file' read as *creating* a file rather than staging
%   an existing one; a preference for `git add .'/`git commit -a' that hides
%   which files are tracked; remote-prefix confusion (origin/branch vs origin
%   branch); and a "blind-testing" effect in which, lacking a mental model,
%   learners just try commands out.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search "challenges confusions version control" -p dblp
% PICKED: the standard reference for Git learning difficulties in a course
%   setting (survey + teacher observations across several courses); primary
%   source for the branch-as-directory misconception and for several
%   command-level confusions (Secs. 5.1--5.7).
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.2, p.10 of author copy): "if asked to checkout a particular
%   branch, learners might try to move into a branch with the cd command. In
%   order to avoid this confusion, the topic should be explicitly discussed.
%   This challenge can be in part attributed to the use of the command line
%   client---hence, in such a setting we stress the importance of showing
%   visual diagrams of branching and explicitly explaining branches as
%   pointers in the commit history."
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.3): "We have noticed a tendency where the command git add
%   file is, however, associated with creating a new file, while it stages the
%   file for the commit. [...] Considered together with the git commit
%   command, which usually ensues the add commands, it is admittedly easy to
%   think that `one creates a new file and then commits it'."
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.1): "Student with little previous command line (unix)
%   experience easily confuse Git commands with Bash commands, and the other
%   way around."
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.4): "In referring to remote branches, this `with and without
%   prefix' conventions confuses learners until they have used the system long
%   enough and grasped the need for specifying the remote context with a
%   prefix".
% QUOTE (Sec. 5.7): "The absence of a proper mental model seems to cause the
%   blind-testing effect [...] learners do not think about the Git commands,
%   but `just try them out', which makes it very difficult to interpret the
%   responses and error messages by the Git client."
% VERIFIED: full text re-read (open author copy, JYU repository,
%   https://jyx.jyu.fi/bitstreams/71186a3c-fc91-4a00-b3e2-990dade59464/download);
%   each quote entails the specific claim it backs (branch-as-directory,
%   add-as-create-file, Git/Bash mixing, remote-prefix, blind-testing).
%   Resolves introtools #3.
% DATE: 2026-07-18
@inproceedings{IsomottonenCochez2014,
  title={Challenges and Confusions in Learning Version Control with {Git}},
  author={Isom{\"o}tt{\"o}nen, Ville and Cochez, Michael},
  booktitle={Information and Communication Technologies in Education, Research, and Industrial Applications},
  series={Communications in Computer and Information Science},
  volume={469},
  publisher={Springer},
  year={2014},
  pages={178--193},
  doi={10.1007/978-3-319-13206-8_9},
}

